PART I VERDICT: both limbs PASS on the pre-repair instrument.
                This is NOT the gate's discharge -- see PART II.
================================================================================

Recorded for the next reader: the disarmed cell is NOT part of this census and
must not be added to it. On a disarmed cell the sampler runs IDENTICALLY -- same
10 s cadence, same scrape, same row-writing cadence, so its cost stays
common-mode and the armed-vs-disarmed control still varies exactly one thing --
but it writes its row with the prune-record columns EMPTY by design, and no
column check reads them. Series absence there is the ARMING WITNESS, checked
once and fail-closed; it is never an instrument defect. This census is over the
ARMED cell, where the completeness gate binds.

================================================================================
PART II -- THE RE-CAPTURE AT THE FINAL INSTRUMENT (THIS IS THE DISCHARGE)
================================================================================

WHY THIS PART IS THE ONE THAT COUNTS. The licensed-backlog repair landed after
Part I was captured. It changed `drain_prunable`'s signature and moved the
eligible/ineligible split to the NEAR side of the removal loop -- the drain path
this record is emitted from. Whether that change can reintroduce a blank cell is
answerable by reading the diff, and reading the diff is precisely what R12
refuses to accept as a discharge. So both limbs were re-run end-to-end against
binaries built from the final tree.

BUILD IDENTITY OF THIS CAPTURE. Working tree clean; server binary and
soak_harness bench binary were produced by

  SDKROOT=$(/usr/bin/xcrun --sdk macosx --show-sdk-path) \
  cargo build --release --bin topgun-server --bench soak_harness

and their link times, read back afterwards by the runner's own resolution
recipe (`spec356-prune.sh`, binary-resolution block), are:

  server binary: target/release/topgun-server
    built:       2026-08-08T08:37:57Z
  soak binary:   target/release/deps/soak_harness-9abf891ab72dea71
    built:       2026-08-08T08:48:33Z
  BUILD_GAP:     636s          (the runner's window is 600s)

CORRECTION -- THIS BLOCK WAS WRONG, AND IS CORRECTED RATHER THAN OVERWRITTEN.
It previously read: "with 60s between their link times -- inside the runner's
own 600s same-build window, so it does not warn." BOTH halves are false. The
gap is 636s, which EXCEEDS the 600s window, so the branch that fires on this
pair is the runner's "probably NOT from one build" WARNING, not the silent
path. The 60s came from comparing the bench binary against ITS OWN `.d` file
(11:47:31 -> 11:48:33 local = 62s) -- the wrong pair. The runner compares the
bench binary against the SERVER binary, which is the 636s above. The error is
recorded here instead of being quietly replaced, on the same rule this
artifact applies to every other finding against its own text: an evidence log
that silently repairs its own false claims is worth less than one that shows
the repair.

WHAT THIS BLOCK THEREFORE NO LONGER CLAIMS. It does not claim the two binaries
came from ONE cargo invocation. With the gap outside the window, the runner's
own heuristic declines that inference and nothing else recorded here
establishes it. The discharge does not rest on it. It rests on two facts that
stand without it:

  (a) BOTH LINK TIMES POSTDATE THE FIX. Both binaries were linked 2026-08-08.
      The licensed-backlog repair whose landing is the entire reason for this
      re-capture was committed 2026-08-04 22:19 +0300 -- four days earlier
      (branch-local pre-merge hash f76f3208, subject "fix(sf-356a): sample the
      licensed backlog before the drain consumes it"; the subject is recorded
      alongside the hash because the hash does not survive a rebase). Neither
      binary can predate the fix, whatever their relationship to each other.

  (b) BEHAVIOURAL FINGERPRINT -- the stronger of the two. The census below
      reads eligible_refs max=1000. The pre-fix code recomputed that quantity
      only AFTER an unbounded drain and could therefore only ever read 0,
      which is exactly what Part I reads (max=0). A binary that prints 1000 is
      not the pre-fix binary. This is a property of the running code rather
      than of a file mtime, so it survives every objection available against
      (a).

The reader who wants to know WHICH tree: it is this spec's final content
commit, whose SHA is resolved by SPEC-356b from `git log main` per R11.1. No
lineage SHA is written here, because writing one here would be the
self-referential pin R0.1 forbids.

